{"prompt": "A shirt costs $25 after a 20% discount was applied twice. What was the original price?", "response": "Let me work through this carefully. Two successive 20% discounts means the price was multiplied by 0.8 twice: original x 0.8 x 0.8 = original x 0.64. So original = 25 / 0.64 = 39.0625. Check: 39.0625 x 0.8 = 31.25, and 31.25 x 0.8 = 25. Correct. The original price was $39.06 (to the cent)."}
{"prompt": "If all Bloops are Razzies and no Razzies are Lazzies, can a Bloop be a Lazzie?", "response": "Step 1: every Bloop is a Razzie. Step 2: no Razzie is a Lazzie, so the sets Razzie and Lazzie are disjoint. Step 3: since Bloops sit entirely inside Razzies, and Razzies never overlap Lazzies, Bloops cannot overlap Lazzies either. So no — a Bloop cannot be a Lazzie."}
{"prompt": "A train leaves Station A at 60 mph. Two hours later, another train leaves the same station going 90 mph in the same direction. When will the second train catch up?", "response": "Let's think step by step. When the second train starts, the first train has already traveled for 2 hours at 60 mph, so it is 120 miles ahead. The second train travels at 90 mph while the first continues at 60 mph, so the gap closes at 90 - 60 = 30 mph. To close a 120-mile gap at 30 mph takes 120/30 = 4 hours. So the second train catches up 4 hours after it departs (which is 6 hours after the first train left). Check: Train 1 has gone 6 x 60 = 360 miles. Train 2 has gone 4 x 90 = 360 miles. They match. Answer: 4 hours after the second train departs."}
{"prompt": "What comes next in the sequence: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, ___?", "response": "Let me analyze this sequence carefully. I'll check the differences between consecutive terms: 3-2=1, 5-3=2, 7-5=2, 11-7=4, 13-11=2. These differences (1, 2, 2, 4, 2) don't form an obvious pattern. Let me try another approach. Looking at the numbers themselves: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13. These are all prime numbers! They're listed in ascending order. The next prime after 13 is 17 (14 is divisible by 2 and 7, 15 by 3 and 5, 16 by 2, but 17 has no divisors other than 1 and itself). Answer: 17."}
{"prompt": "You have a 3-gallon jug and a 5-gallon jug. How can you measure exactly 4 gallons of water?", "response": "I'll work through this systematically. My goal is to end up with exactly 4 gallons in one of the jugs. Here's my step-by-step plan: Step 1: Fill the 5-gallon jug completely (5 gal, 0 gal). Step 2: Pour from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug until the 3-gallon jug is full (2 gal remaining in 5-gallon, 3 gal in 3-gallon). Step 3: Empty the 3-gallon jug (2 gal, 0 gal). Step 4: Pour the 2 gallons from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug (0 gal, 2 gal). Step 5: Fill the 5-gallon jug completely (5 gal, 2 gal). Step 6: Pour from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug until full. The 3-gallon jug needs 1 more gallon (it has 2), so we pour 1 gallon from the 5-gallon jug. This leaves exactly 4 gallons in the 5-gallon jug (5-1=4). Answer: 4 gallons in the 5-gallon jug."}
{"prompt": "A recipe calls for 3/4 cup of sugar. I want to make 2/3 of the recipe. How much sugar do I need?", "response": "To find 2/3 of 3/4 cup, I need to multiply these fractions: (2/3) x (3/4). When multiplying fractions, multiply the numerators together and the denominators together: (2 x 3) / (3 x 4) = 6/12. Now I can simplify 6/12 by dividing both by their greatest common divisor, which is 6: 6/6 = 1 and 12/6 = 2. So 6/12 = 1/2. Alternative check: I could cancel the 3s first: (2/3) x (3/4) = 2 x 3 / 3 x 4 = 2/4 = 1/2. Answer: 1/2 cup of sugar."}
{"prompt": "If it takes 5 machines 5 minutes to make 5 widgets, how long would it take 100 machines to make 100 widgets?", "response": "This is a classic trick question, so let me reason carefully. First, let's figure out the rate of production. If 5 machines make 5 widgets in 5 minutes, that means each machine makes 1 widget in 5 minutes (since 5 widgets / 5 machines = 1 widget per machine). So the rate is 1 widget per machine per 5 minutes. Now, with 100 machines, each working independently at the same rate, each machine will make 1 widget in 5 minutes. So 100 machines will make 100 widgets in... 5 minutes. The key insight is that the number of machines and the number of widgets scale proportionally, so the time stays the same. Answer: 5 minutes."}
